WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:05.280
Today, I'm going to be speaking with Dan Kovalik,
who is a labor and human rights attorney and

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:12.290
also author of the book, canceled this book,
The Progressive Case Against Council Culture.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.270
It's great to have you on down.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:15.440
It's great to be here, David.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.440
Thank you.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:22.330
So the reason I'm interested in this is there
are certainly a range of views across the

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:27.230
political spectrum about this issue of so-called
council culture and what what counts as council

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:28.880
culture and what doesn't.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.329
But even within the left, there is some disagreement
as to whether it's taking place, what counts,

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:37.050
what should be done.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:43.210
Maybe start by kind of giving us your sense
of relative to the idea that we hear on right

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:51.699
wing media about this as a sort of speech
suppression, politically based epidemic that

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:58.910
is taking place in media and academia and
across the social and cultural areas.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:02.640
How does your position compare to that as
a starting point?

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:10.270
Yes, to compare it to the right wing position,
I think mine is certainly different.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.000
First of all, I'm not hysterical about it.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.410
I mean, I, I try to deal with it in a reasoned
way.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.990
I certainly am concerned about it.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:26.070
You know, I'll just give examples like I'm
not concerned about the fact that Pepy Lupu

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.200
is not going to be in the next this movie.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.390
Yeah.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.890
That was decided by the producers.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:40.540
And, you know, I certainly think that he is
a problematic character who basically his

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:45.890
only thing he ever did was assault the other
skunk.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.260
The same thing with the Dr. Seuss issue.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:56.750
I mean, the state itself, apparently, of Dr.
Seuss decided to get rid of these six books,

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.200
which no one ever heard of and which, you
know, were offensive.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.470
And I think Dr. Seuss.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.130
Progressed in his thoughts as he got older.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:14.840
And that actually is a good Segway into what
I am concerned about, what would concern me

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:21.410
is if all of Dr. Seuss's books were canceled
because of his earlier works.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.690
OK, the concern I have about.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:34.400
Cancer culture, particularly on the left,
is that it seems to me there's a lack of forgiveness

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.330
and and chance for redemption for people.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.890
You make one mistake and I've seen this, all
of us have seen this many times.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.320
And that's why people who deny the cancer
culture exist.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.300
I just think we're not being honest.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:54.400
We see many times someone will do something
that is offensive or barely offensive or seen

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.970
by someone is offensive and they're immediately
shut out.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.840
People call for them to be fired.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.780
Sometimes they'll get fired.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:05.780
That concerns me.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.620
So let's break down that process so that we
can maybe even figure out which is the part

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:15.850
that concerns you, because so let's imagine
a situation where someone tweets something

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.600
that we agree is offensive, not related to
their work while they are on the weekend.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.870
Or, you know, there was this story that Jon
Ronson talks about in his book.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.709
So you've been publicly shamed, a woman who
tweeted something about white versus black

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:31.490
people getting AIDS as she stepped onto a
flight.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:35.900
And then when she landed and it exploded into
an international story and photographers were

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.380
waiting for her and whatever.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:46.709
OK, so the thing is said then there is a public
outcry from people who say this is bad.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.730
Sometimes you'll see the public contact the
employer of the individual and ask for them

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.180
to be fired.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.760
Even if what was said does does arguably not
directly relate to the person's job, the employer

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.880
will then sometimes put out a statement.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:04.830
Sometimes they'll say, we are disciplining
the person.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.630
Sometimes they will fire the person.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:13.110
Now, at every individual level, people tweeting
outrage seems to be something that I don't

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:14.110
have a problem with.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:19.479
People tweeting outrage, people demanding
that the person be fired is a question mark

00:04:19.479 --> 00:04:20.479
for me.

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:25.729
And this this has been done to me before,
in fact, as well, in the sense that certainly

00:04:25.729 --> 00:04:30.139
calling a business, if you find out the person
works somewhere and you're going to say if

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:34.270
this person works for you, I'm not going to
be a patron of your business, certainly something

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.580
I can't say is wrong to do, although I might
disagree with it.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.720
And then the firing decision.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.610
An employer can decide as long as they're
not discriminating on the basis of protected

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:50.760
class, it's no longer good for our business
to have this person working for us.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:56.990
So like the pieces all when you break them
down, the way I'm doing it aren't particularly

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:57.990
concerning.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.960
So, like, which aspect is that you are most
worried about?

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.440
Well, first of all, I mean, the context is
everything.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.590
And I mean, it's one thing if you just if
you find out someone is an avowed white supremacist,

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:17.110
OK, at night, dresses up in a hood and is
part of the Ku Klux Klan.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:21.469
This is a person with bad motives, bad intentions.

00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:28.360
I think how you deal with that person would
be different than someone who who is otherwise

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:34.779
well-intentioned, who says something inadvertently,
you're offensive or does something inadvertently

00:05:34.779 --> 00:05:36.580
offensive.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.710
I think they need to be treated differently.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:47.419
I don't think you should pile on someone,
even on social media who makes a mistake and

00:05:47.419 --> 00:05:48.550
who says, look, I'm sorry.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:49.550
Right?

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:50.550
And that happens a lot.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.550
Right.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.979
And usually even after the I'm sorry, it doesn't
end right.

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:58.969
And I certainly don't think you should try
to get someone like that fired except in the

00:05:58.969 --> 00:06:00.930
most egregious circumstance.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:07.889
I don't see getting someone fired in most
circumstances, some act of social activism.

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:15.419
I see it as something counter to solidarity
with your fellow workers, with your fellow

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:16.889
comrades.

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:22.699
Many times in this concerns me greatly, especially
in the academy now.

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:24.119
And I talk about this in my book.

00:06:24.119 --> 00:06:30.509
I think a lot of people don't understand that
the academy has gone through a huge transformation

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:37.629
in recent decades from tenured professors
on the most part to now adjunct professor.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:39.809
Most professors now are adjunct.

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:40.809
What does that mean?

00:06:40.809 --> 00:06:43.759
Means they don't have tenure, the right job
security.

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:46.169
They get paid poverty wages.

00:06:46.169 --> 00:06:48.719
I'm an adjunct.

00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:51.479
For example, I get three thousand dollars
per class.

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:52.580
I teach at the law school.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:58.279
I may teach 50 students to pay 40 grand a
year to attend it.

00:06:58.279 --> 00:06:59.279
Right.

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:04.559
And I get three grand for the whole class
and can be fired mid semester.

00:07:04.559 --> 00:07:10.289
I have no you know, I'm basically a glorified,
you know, gig worker.

00:07:10.289 --> 00:07:11.469
Right, right.

00:07:11.469 --> 00:07:12.469
Yeah.

00:07:12.469 --> 00:07:15.529
And I've taught adjunct as well at Boston
College and I've had a similar experience.

00:07:15.529 --> 00:07:22.219
And to call lightly and I think many times
it's slightly for someone like that to be

00:07:22.219 --> 00:07:24.110
fired.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.020
Is kicking down.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:30.610
It's not punching up, it's not fighting, you're
not taking on the man, in fact, I agree with

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:31.610
you.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:33.169
He is uniting with the man, right?

00:07:33.169 --> 00:07:38.899
The administrators who make a lot of money,
hundreds of thousands of dollars, if not over

00:07:38.899 --> 00:07:42.199
a million against these lowly adjuncts.

00:07:42.199 --> 00:07:44.779
And you see this.

00:07:44.779 --> 00:07:49.399
A lot,
yeah, a woman named Amy Siskind, a Democratic

00:07:49.399 --> 00:07:54.689
activist, tried to get me fired for from Boston
College because I responded to a tweet of

00:07:54.689 --> 00:08:01.960
hers saying that the 20 20 nominee or maybe
it was even 2016, 2020 shouldn't be white

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:02.960
or a man.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.949
And I said, that seems to me to be wrong to
to disqualify someone on the basis of sex

00:08:07.949 --> 00:08:08.949
or race.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:10.720
And she tried to get me fired from Boston
College.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:11.830
And that's wrong.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:18.979
But what I'm hearing from you is more that
you just think sometimes these tools are wielded

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:22.699
when they shouldn't be like there is more
egregious and less egregious cases.

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:27.800
And sometimes you just think the action doesn't
rise to warranting a call to be fired.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.080
For example,
I think it would be a rare case where that

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.300
would be legitimate.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:33.959
Let me put it that way.

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:38.849
But I do think there's something that people
now refer to the Overton Window.

00:08:38.849 --> 00:08:43.889
Yes, you've probably heard that, that there
is a line.

00:08:43.889 --> 00:08:45.540
There are lines of demarcation.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:55.430
Again, I think if someone is an avowed racist,
an avowed white supremacist, you know, particularly

00:08:55.430 --> 00:09:04.720
in a sensitive position of power, I think
it's legitimate to call that person out publicly

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.240
and with their employer.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.850
But who gets to who gets to decide what rises
to what level?

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:11.500
And the position is sensitive.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:12.520
Well, that's a great question.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:19.430
I mean, I think obviously it's up to the individual
who's doing the attempted counseling, if we

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:24.040
want to say that, OK, has to use some judgment.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:29.430
But I think giving what I'm illustrating is
that you would do this very rarely.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:34.050
You know, there's you know, not that many.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:37.490
There are obviously avowed white supremacists.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:43.529
But, you know, most of the people we come
in contact with on a daily basis are not that.

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:48.230
And in most of the people that are being canceled
are not that.

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:55.279
In fact, you know, the irony is to calling
someone a racist or sexist is only damaging

00:09:55.279 --> 00:09:57.800
to people who don't want to be racist or sexist.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:58.800
Right.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.290
I mean, if you're an avowed racist, that's
a compliment, I guess.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:06.490
So what would be what would be an example
of someone who was called a racist and then

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:07.490
fired?

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:10.779
But what they said was just a lapse in judgment
and really they shouldn't have been.

00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:17.750
OK, well, I'll give an example from my book
and why I wrote the book because of something

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:23.240
that happened in Pittsburgh in this was not
someone who was fired, but in a way, it was

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.550
it was even greater than being fired.

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:31.750
So in Pittsburgh, we have this peace and justice
group called the Thomas Merton Center, and

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:37.879
it was founded 50 years ago, co-founded by
someone named Molly Rush, a long time peace

00:10:37.879 --> 00:10:46.540
activist and also someone who's advocated
for economic and racial justice during the

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:52.279
George FOID protest of last summer, she posted
on Facebook, reposted.

00:10:52.279 --> 00:10:58.079
Someone had posted it and she just hit share,
which many of us do without thinking.

00:10:58.079 --> 00:11:05.810
She posted this meme with a picture of Martin
Luther King and it said, Never rioted, never

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:06.810
looted.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.430
Change the world.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:16.129
Immediately, people started jumping on her
on Facebook, attacking her as a racist, attacking

00:11:16.129 --> 00:11:21.700
her family who defended her, calling them
white trash.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:26.480
She apologized on Facebook publicly, but that
wasn't enough.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.500
What what ended up happening happening is
the Thomas Merton Center, the board of the

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.020
Thomas Merton Center, put out a letter.

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:42.379
Both to all Thomas Merton Center members and
publicly on Facebook, saying they could no

00:11:42.379 --> 00:11:44.470
longer work with Molly Rush.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.810
Yeah, after 50 years.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:53.760
And this is, you know, so the argument was
this meme was racist because you shouldn't

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.649
tell African-Americans how to fight for their
liberation, right?

00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:03.129
If if there's some violence that ensues, it's
not for us to say.

00:12:03.129 --> 00:12:06.380
And of course, though, as I point out in my
book.

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:12.139
If you look at a lot of the violence that
happened, a lot of it.

00:12:12.139 --> 00:12:18.259
Probably the vast majority was not done by
African-Americans, it was done either by young

00:12:18.259 --> 00:12:26.870
white anarchists, usually young white males
or young white male right wingers trying to

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:32.629
either inflame the situation or to delegitimize
Black Lives Matter.

00:12:32.629 --> 00:12:37.370
So calling those people out.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:42.259
Why is that problematic, in fact, in Portland,
as I mentioned, there were newspaper articles

00:12:42.259 --> 00:12:48.949
that quoted people from Black Lives Matter
and from the NAACP saying that the violence

00:12:48.949 --> 00:12:55.839
that took place night after night in Portland
and around midnight and later had nothing

00:12:55.839 --> 00:12:57.190
to do with Black Lives Matter.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:58.339
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:58.339 --> 00:12:59.339
Something else.

00:12:59.339 --> 00:13:05.930
This was, again, young white people who were
anarchists or whatever they were who decided

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:10.610
they wanted to do what they wanted to do,
which was attack the federal courthouse night

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:11.610
after night.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:12.920
I think this is a great example.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.509
I mean, listen, if this example is as you
describe it, and I have no reason to doubt

00:13:16.509 --> 00:13:20.360
it, I'm just saying I'm what I know about
it is what you're telling me exactly.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:25.319
This would be one where I agree this this
sounds like the wrong solution.

00:13:25.319 --> 00:13:29.790
But in a I guess where I worry is that in
a country of three hundred and thirty one

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:33.259
million people, there is examples of everything.

00:13:33.259 --> 00:13:38.160
Every good company that's a big company is
going to have one hundred or a thousand customer

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:43.089
experiences that sound terrible, but they
represent very little in the scope of the

00:13:43.089 --> 00:13:44.370
how the company deals with customers.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:52.379
As an example, you know, I worry that the
this is being called an epidemic when I will

00:13:52.379 --> 00:13:55.620
grant that in a country of three hundred and
thirty one million.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.370
The example just you gave me sounds terrible.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:01.889
It sounds completely counterproductive to
the goals of me as a person on the left, for

00:14:01.889 --> 00:14:02.889
example.

00:14:02.889 --> 00:14:03.889
And I'm with you.

00:14:03.889 --> 00:14:06.670
If the details are as described, I just don't
see the epidemic.

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:11.579
And individually, it sounds like you certainly
defend the people, the right of people to

00:14:11.579 --> 00:14:13.819
express their distaste on social media.

00:14:13.819 --> 00:14:14.819
Right.

00:14:14.819 --> 00:14:16.360
Like you and I are on the same page there.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:17.889
Yes, absolutely.

00:14:17.889 --> 00:14:23.259
And you certainly defend the right of individuals
to tell a company I won't do business with

00:14:23.259 --> 00:14:27.649
you because of this, whether they're right
or wrong, you defend the right that they have

00:14:27.649 --> 00:14:28.749
to do that.

00:14:28.749 --> 00:14:31.040
Well, certainly consumers have that right.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:32.439
They certainly have that legal right.

00:14:32.439 --> 00:14:33.439
They should.

00:14:33.439 --> 00:14:34.439
Yeah.

00:14:34.439 --> 00:14:35.899
Again, it's a matter of discretion.

00:14:35.899 --> 00:14:36.899
Sure.

00:14:36.899 --> 00:14:41.699
Whether or a matter of conscience, whether
they should do it, that's another question.

00:14:41.699 --> 00:14:42.699
But.

00:14:42.699 --> 00:14:43.699
Right.

00:14:43.699 --> 00:14:44.850
People have the legal right to do that.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:50.110
And then businesses as far as businesses that
exist, some are regulated in certain ways

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:51.110
and some some are others.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:55.060
As long as they're not breaking any laws,
they can also decide having a certain employee

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:57.760
is bad for us and it could hurt profits or
whatever.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.920
We don't want this employee working for us
that you agree with.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:03.089
Yes, of course.

00:15:03.089 --> 00:15:09.960
So it sounds like really it's the sometimes
overbearing activation of these tactics that

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.009
you're concerned about.

00:15:11.009 --> 00:15:12.009
Well, yes.

00:15:12.009 --> 00:15:17.389
And again, I never use the word epidemic in
my book or when I talk about this.

00:15:17.389 --> 00:15:18.389
Yeah.

00:15:18.389 --> 00:15:20.089
And I wouldn't use that.

00:15:20.089 --> 00:15:29.079
But I do think it is I think it is a frequent
use tactic against people, particularly in

00:15:29.079 --> 00:15:30.079
the academy.

00:15:30.079 --> 00:16:38.269
The academies really soaked in 

00:16:38.269 --> 00:16:39.459
this stuff.

00:16:39.459 --> 00:17:10.170
Let's pause our conversation there 
with Daniel Kovalik, author of 

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:27.540
Canceled 

